# SHA-256 of every native library this app ships for arm64-v8a.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (#437 review): the prebuilt .so files are the app's trusted
# computing base and the only carrier of the MLS security/correctness behaviour, but
# nothing in this repo recorded WHICH binary was supposed to be here. A reviewer
# could not tell an intended rebuild from a silent swap. This manifest is that
# record; `__tests__/nativeProvenance.test.ts` enforces it on every CI run, so
# changing a binary without consciously updating this file fails the gate.
#
# Verify by hand:
#   cd android/app/src/main/jniLibs/arm64-v8a && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
#
# These are the hashes of the files AS CHECKED IN. Verifying against a release APK
# instead: liblogoschat.so, liblogosdelivery.so and librln.so pass through packaging
# BIT-IDENTICAL (they are already fully stripped), so their in-APK hashes match this
# file directly. liblogoschat_bridge.so and libc++_shared.so still carry a symbol
# table, which AGP's strip pass removes while packaging — their in-APK hashes DIFFER
# by design. Cross-check those two by GNU build-id instead (`readelf -n <lib>`):
#   liblogoschat_bridge.so  9c3ab5ab86357ee11e9751c10414151e0cfae804
#   libc++_shared.so        6783a7f3a0d9c67c55a74cadc441ed55aaa493da
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROVENANCE — one machine-checked record per shipped library.
#
# SECOND #437 REVIEW ROUND. Recording a hash next to the binary only proves the
# manifest agrees with itself. It does NOT answer "was this binary produced by the
# source revision we claim?" — and at the previous head it demonstrably was not
# answerable: this app shipped liblogoschat.so 6dd23bc7…, while the native revision
# cited here (@6b6305f) still PUBLISHED 8f4fbdc6…. The cited revision stood behind a
# different binary than the one under review.
#
# Fixed at the source: xAlisher/logos-libchat-mls-android@a81464c now publishes the
# exact artifact this app bundles, and the `published=` field below is the hash that
# revision publishes. `__tests__/nativeProvenance.test.ts` asserts published= equals
# the recorded hash equals the bytes on disk, for every library — so this manifest
# can no longer cite a revision that stands behind something else.
#
# THIRD #437 REVIEW ROUND — the same drift, in the other direction. The gate above
# works: it is what caught this. Two late commits rebuilt liblogoschat.so for the
# creator-gated replace-on-desync path (4d13f90 → 6b12a4fb) and shipped the new
# binary WITHOUT touching this file, so the manifest still named 6dd23bc7 and the
# whole PR shipped an unverified TCB. The lesson is not "add another check" — the
# check fired — it is that a .so bump is a THREE-repo edit: publish the artifact
# upstream, move `published=` + the recorded hash here, and re-anchor the in-binary
# marker in the gate. 6b12a4fb carries the SAME 25 exported logoschat_* symbols as
# 6dd23bc7, so nothing about the ABI would have told you; only the hash does.
#
# FOURTH #437 REVIEW ROUND — the same defect a third time, and the worst shape of
# it. bd4b229 bumped liblogoschat.so to 84b751c2 (the signing-key-gated build) and
# in the SAME commit reverted this file, docs/SBOM.md, the marker assertions and
# __tests__/nativeSbomDoc.test.ts back to their round-2 text. So the manifest named
# 6dd23bc7 — the UNGATED build — beside a binary two security rounds newer, and the
# round-3 hardening that would have shouted about it had been deleted along the way.
# Two lessons, both structural:
#   1. A .so bump reverting its own manifest is indistinguishable from a downgrade
#      attack. Only the gate firing tells them apart, so never "reconcile" the
#      manifest toward the binary or the binary toward the manifest without
#      re-deciding which build you mean to ship.
#   2. A deleted test is a silent weakening — nothing fails when a gate is removed
#      from jest.logic.config.js. __tests__/nativeGateRegistration.test.ts now pins
#      that registration, because that is the one hole markers and hashes can't
#      cover.
# And the upstream half had been skipped outright: 01fced9 changed the gate's SOURCE
# but published nothing, so for this whole review round the app bundled 84b751c2, an
# artifact NO revision of the native repo stood behind. @a81464c publishes it now.
#
# What NO in-binary marker can tell you here, stated plainly: 6b12a4fb and 84b751c2
# emit the SAME log lines and export the SAME 25 logoschat_* symbols. They differ
# only in what the creator gate compares — credential CONTENT bytes (6b12a4fb) vs the
# welcome author's authenticated `signature_key` (84b751c2, so a peer that copies the
# creator's id into a credential can no longer pass). The markers below separate
# gated from ungated; only this hash separates the two gated builds.
#
# LIMIT, stated plainly: the CI logic job is offline, so the gate proves the three
# local facts agree, not that the remote still publishes that hash. Checking the
# remote is a network step — `scripts/verify-native-provenance.sh` does exactly that
# and is the command a reviewer should run.
#
# Format (parsed by the gate — keep it exact):
#   # provenance: <file> <origin> published=<sha256>
#
